## 1. Introduction
Among many energy storage devices, aqueous zinc ion batteries (ZIBs) have aroused increasing interest due to their advantages of high power density, environmental friendliness, abundant resources, low cost, high safety and reliability [1–3]. Besides, zinc metal possesses excellent theoretical capacity up to $820\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and a relatively low redox potential $\ensuremath{\langle-0.76\vee}$ vs SHE) [4–6]. However, the strong electrostatic interaction between zinc ions and the matrix structure leads to rapid capacity decay of cathode material [7], which seriously affects the commercialization of ZIBs. Therefore, it is very important to find suitable cathode materials for the development of ZIBs.
At present, manganese oxide based electrode materials are considered as potential cathodes for ZIBs due to their advantages of high redox potential, low cost, high nature abundance and green environmental protection [8,9]. For example, $\mathsf{\alpha}\mathsf{a}\mathsf{-}\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}\mathsf{O}_{2}$ [10–14], d$\ensuremath{\mathrm{MnO}_{2}}$ [15–20], $\mathrm{Mn}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ [21], $\scriptstyle\mathrm{{Mn}}_{2}\mathrm{{O}}_{3}$ [22–25], and $\mathrm{ZnMn}_{2}{\cal O}_{4}$ [26– 29] have been widely studied as zinc storage cathode materials. In the past, MnO was considered to be largely unable to store zinc ions. The reason is that MnO has low conductivity, as well as poor structural stability in the process of the zinc ions intercalation and deintercalation. Recently, Wang et al. showed that the inert MnO was converted to layered-type $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MnO}_{2}}$ by electrochemically activating during the initial charging process. It shows the capacity was $95~\mathrm{mAh}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at a current density of $1000\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and the capacity retention was $80.7\ \%$ (after 300 cycles) at a current density of $300\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{\g}^{-1}$ [30]. However, its shortcomings such as poor conductivity, manganese dissolution, and volume expansion have not been well solved, resulting in inferior rate performance and cycling stability. Therefore, there are still a lot of room for improving the electrochemical performance of MnO cathodes in ZIBs.
Carbon coating is considered to be one of the most effective strategies to solve the problem of poor conductivity of electrode materials. Chen et al. used a hydrothermal method to synthesize $\mathrm{Mn}_{3}0_{4}@C$ as cathode material. Compared with pure $\mathrm{Mn}_{3}{\cal O}_{4}$ cathode, the $\mathrm{Mn}_{3}0_{4}@C$ cathode possessed higher rate performance. The results showed that the conductivity of the cathode was remarkably improved, benefiting from the uniform carbon coating layer [31]. Liu et al. synthesized ${\mathsf{V O}}_{2}@{\mathsf{C}}$ by a hydrothermal method, also proved that amorphous carbon could improve the conductivity of $\mathsf{V O}_{2}$ cathode material [32]. Carbon-coated precursors are usually produced using a hydrothermal technique with dopamine [33,34] or glucose [32,35,36] as carbon sources. However, the hydrothermal reaction necessitates high temperature and pressure, and the synthetic conditions are relatively harsh. In general, the self-assembly synthesis of metal organic frameworks (MOFs) as a carbon source at room temperature is more in line with commercialization criteria in terms of economic and energy savings. In addition, MOFs materials not only have the advantages of large specific surface area, porous structure, high thermal and chemical stability [37], but also could transfer these advantages to their derivatives. It is worth mentioning that there has been a substantial amount of scientific research that adequately demonstrate the unique advantages of MOFs as a carbon source in the field of electrode material preparation [38–42].
The construction of heterostructures to improve the structural stability of electrode materials would be a beneficial strategy. Among many metal oxides, transition metal oxides possess abundant electrical and magnetic properties [43–46], which are commonly utilized in the fabrication of heterostructures to enhance the structural stability of electrode materials. He et al. reported the preparation of $\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}\mathsf{-C o M o O}_{4}$ by the solvothermal process. At the same time, they demonstrated that the heterogeneous structure generated by $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and ${\mathsf{C o M o O}}_{4}$ could efficiently prevent the structural deterioration of anode material throughout the Li-ion embedding and stripping processes [47]. Zeng et al. also proved that $\mathrm{Mn_{2}O_{3}–Z n M n_{2}O_{4}}$ heterostructure possesses superior structural stability [28]. Notably, rare-earth metal oxides $(\mathsf{C e O}_{2})$ could likewise improve the structural stability of electrode materials [48]. Interestingly, ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ also has excellent redox properties $(\mathsf{C e}^{3+}/$ ${\mathsf{C e}}^{4+}.$ ) [49], high thermal stability, abundant oxygen vacancies, environmental protection, and low cost [50,51]. Furthermore, Tang et al. demonstrated that $\mathsf{C e O}_{\mathrm{x}}$ $\mathrm{^{\prime}C e^{3+}\leftrightarrow C e^{4+^{\cdot}}}.$ ) could possess the reversible surface oxygen ion exchange and be provided with good electronic/ionic conductivities [52].
Herein, we constructed a hollow composite of $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}/\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}\ @\mathsf C$ derived from the self-assembly of Ce-Mn-metal organic frameworks (Ce-Mn-MOFs) in which the carbon layer comes from the carbonization of the organics in the framework at high temperatures in an inert atmosphere. This unique design has the following advantages: first, the interaction between ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ and the $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}$ interface slows down the dissolution of manganese, thus ensuring good mechanical stability of the structure; second, the hollow structure presents unique advantages of high specific surface areas, as well as allows for intimate penetration of electrolyte and rapid electronic/ionic transfer; third, it effectively utilizes the distinctive properties of rare-earth and broadens the application range of rareearth in the field of electrode material fabrication. In a 3 M $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{CF}_{3^{-}}$ $S0_{3})_{2}+0.1$ M $\mathrm{{MnSO}_{4}}$ aqueous electrolyte system, the obtained composite electrode material has better cycling stability and superior rate performance at a high current density of $500\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for ZIBs compared with pure $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}$ . Specifically, the $1\ \%\ C e0_{2}/\mathrm{MnO}_{\times}@.$ C composite cathode shows a remarkable reversible specific capacity of $130\mathrm{\mAh/g}$ , 6.5 times more than the pure $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}$ $(20\mathrm{mAh}/\mathrm{g})$ at a current density of $500\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . The capacity retention is up to $99.5\ \%$ relative to the initial capacity after 800 cycles.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Material
Polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP, $\mathrm{M}_{\mathrm{w}}=58000$ , K29-32), manganese acetate tetrahydrate $(\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{Ac})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , $99.0\%$ , trimesic acid $(98\ \%)$ , cerium nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathsf{C e}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ , $99.5\mathrm{~\%~}$ and manganese chloride tetrachloride $(\mathrm{MnCl}_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , $99.0\mathrm{~\}\%\right)$ were purchased from Aladdin. Urea $'C O(N H_{2})_{2}$ , $99\ \%$ ) was supplied from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., ltd. (Shanghai, China). Ethanol was purchased from Fuyu Fine Chemical Co., ltd. (Tianjin, China). All above-mentioned chemicals were listed in Table S1 and used directly without further purification. Deionized water used in all experiments was purified through a Millipore system $(18.25~\mathrm{M}\Omega)$ .
## 2.2. Synthesis of $C e O_{2}/M n O_{x}@C$
$\mathsf{C e O}_{2}/\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}@\mathsf C$ was synthesized according to a typical reported method.[53] In brief, $490\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{Ac})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , 8.7 mg of ${\mathrm{Ce}}({\mathsf{N O}}_{3})_{3}.$ - - $6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ and $2.0\ {\mathrm{g}}$ PVP were dissolved in a $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ mixed solvent (ethanol/deionized water $2{:}1\ \mathbf{\Sigma}\ \mathbf{v}/\mathbf{v})$ to form clear solution A, ${900}~\mathrm{mg}$ trimesic acid was dissolved in a $80~\mathrm{mL}$ mixed solvent (ethanol/deionized water $2{:}1\ \mathrm{v}/\mathrm{v})$ to form clear solution B. Then, the above solutions A and B were stirred under vigorous stirring for $40\ \mathrm{min}$ , respectively. Afterwards, the solution B was added dropwise into solution A under vigorous stirring at room temperature. After stirring for $20~\mathrm{{min}}$ , the reaction mixture solution was obtained, which was then allowed to rest at room temperature for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The white precipitates were collected by centrifugation, washed with ethanol several times and dried in vacuum oven at $60~^{\circ}C$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize6h}$ . Note the white precipitates were flagged as $1\%$
Using a similar procedure as the described above, the ${\mathrm{MnO}}_{\mathrm{x}}@C$ , $0.5\ \%\ C e{0_{2}}/\mathrm{MnO_{x}}@C$ , $3\ \%\ C e{0_{2}}/{\mathrm{MnO}_{\mathrm{x}}}@C$ and $5\ \%\ C e{0_{2}}/{\mathrm{MnO}_{\mathrm{x}}}\ @C$ were obtained by adjusting the $\mathsf{C e}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ amount to 0, 4.3, 26.1 and $43.2\ \mathrm{mg}$ , respectively.
