## 2.5. Electrochemical measurements
Using $N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -methyl-2-pyrrolidone (NMP) as solvent, the slurry was prepared by evenly mixing the active substances, super $\mathrm{~\bf~P~}$ and polyvinylidene fluoride (PVDF) with a mass ratio of 7:2:1. Then, the obtained slurry was coated on 200 mesh stainless steel mesh (SSM) and dried at $60~^{\circ}C$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ under vacuum oven. Finally, the SSM loaded with active substances was cut into several disk electrodes with a diameter of $12~\mathrm{mm}$ . The CR2032 coin-type cell was assembled using the above disk electrode as cathode, glass microfiber filter (Whatman GF/D) as separator and $Z\mathrm{n}$ foil as anode in air. The electrolyte solution was 3 M $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{CF}_{3}\mathrm{SO}_{3})_{2}$ with $0.1\mathrm{~M~}$ $\mathsf{M n S O}_{4}$ in deionized water. The average mass density of active material in each electrode was around $1.0\mathrm{\mg\cm}^{-2}$ . Each cell was activated for 5 cycles at a current density of $20\mathrm{mAg}^{-1}$ before formal test.
The galvanostatic charge and discharge (GCD) and rate performance were tested at a series of current densities on a battery tester (Wuhan LAND, CT2001A, China) in a voltage range between 0.8 and $1.8{\mathrm{~V~}}$ (vs $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}\rangle$ . Cyclic voltammetry (CV) tests were performed by using a CHI 750E electrochemical workstation (Shanghai Chenhua, China) at various scan rates within the potential window of 0.8 to $1.8\mathrm{V}$ (vs $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn}\rangle$ . On a CHI 750E electrochemical workstation, electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) tests were carried out with an AC amplitude of $5~\mathrm{mV}$ spanning a frequency range of $10^{5}$ to $0.01{\mathrm{Hz}}$ . All electrochemical tests were measured under air at room temperature.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Material characterizations
The synthesis of $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}/\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}@\mathsf C$ was depicted in Scheme 1. The Ce-Mn-MOFs materials were prepared by self-assembly strategy using trimesic acid as the organic ligand, $\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{Ac})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ and Ce $(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ serve as metal ion sources. Then, the Ce-Mn-MOFs materials were calcined at $800^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ for $60\mathrm{min}$ under Ar atmosphere to obtain hollow $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}/\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}@\mathsf C$ . The crystalline phase of the asobtained $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}},$ $\mathrm{MnO}_{\mathrm{x}}@C$ and $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}/\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}@\mathsf C$ were analyzed by XRD. As shown in Fig. 1a, the characteristic diffraction peaks of $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}$ and $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}@\mathsf C$ at $35.0^{\circ}$ , $40.6^{\circ}$ , $58.8^{\circ}$ , $70.3^{\circ}$ and $73.9^{\circ}$ correspond to the (111), (200), (220), (311) and (222) crystal planes,[35] which were in accordance with the face-centered cubic MnO phase (space group $\mathrm{Fm}{-}3~\mathrm{m}$ , PDF#75–0626), respectively. For $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}/\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}@\mathsf C$ , it not only showed the characteristic diffraction peaks consistent with the above MnO phase, but also exhibited other peaks at $28.5^{\circ}$ , $33.1^{\circ}$ , $47.5^{\circ}$ , 56.3, $59.1^{\circ}$ , $69.4^{\circ}$ , $76.7^{\circ}$ and $79.1^{\circ}$ , which were indexed to the.
(1 1 1), (2 0 0), (2 2 0), (3 1 1), (2 2 2), (4 0 0), (3 3 1) and (4 2 0) crystal planes, respectively.[54] They were consistent with the fluoritestructured ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ phase (space group $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Fm}}-3\ensuremath{\mathrm{~m~}}$ , PDF#78–0694). In addition, all of the samples revealed several weakly peaks at $32.4^{\circ}$ , $36.1^{\circ}$ , $44.4^{\circ}~50.8^{\circ}$ and $59.9^{\circ}$ , which were found to be the $\mathrm{Mn}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ phase (space group $F m{-}3\mathrm{~m~}$ , PDF#80–0382),[31] marking by quincunxes. It was indicated that the main component of $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}$ was MnO.
The structural changes of as-prepared materials could be further investigated by Raman spectra. As shown in Fig. 1b, the peaks located at 312.01, 364.57 and $652.06~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were ascribed to the
Mn-O vibrational band. The two peaks located at $1337.94~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (D-band) and $1595.01\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (G-band) correspond to the vibrational modes of amorphous carbon and the graphitic carbon bands, respectively. Interestingly, further calculation found that the D-band and G-band intensity ratios $\left(\mathrm{I_{D}}/\mathrm{I_{G}}\right)$ for $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}@\mathsf C$ and $1\%$ $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}/\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}\ @C$ were determined to be approximately 0.937 and 0.941, respectively. The results indicated that $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}@C$ and $1\%$ $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}/\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}\ @C$ contain highly ordered graphitized carbon, which could improve the conductivity of the electrode material.[35] In contrast, the Raman spectrum of $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}$ only showed the peak of vibration with Mn-O band. As shown in the embedded magnified image of Fig. 1b, $\mathbf{F2g}$ bands located at $466.47~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ represent the vibrational mode of the fluorite-type structure of bulk ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ , further demonstrating the $1~\%~{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}/{\mathsf{M n O}}_{\mathrm{x}}\ @{\mathsf{C}}$ was synthesized successfully. Notably, the Mn-O band red-shifts from $657.83~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ in $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}@\mathsf C$ to $652.06~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ in $1~\%~{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}/{\mathsf{M n O}}_{\mathrm{x}}\ @{\mathsf{C}}$ (Fig. 1c), indicating the strong interaction between $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}$ and ceria-species.[55].
Fig. S1a shows the $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherms of $1\%$ $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}/\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}\ @C$ composite at $77~\mathrm{K}.$ The isotherms of $1\%\ C e O_{2}/$ $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}@\mathsf C$ composite may be classified as IV isotherm with hysteresis loops in the $\mathsf{p}/\mathsf{p}_{0}$ range between 0.45 and 0.95.[56] The BET surface area of the synthesized $1~\%~{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}/{\mathsf{M n O}}_{\mathrm{x}}\varpi C$ electrode is $205~\mathrm{m}^{2}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . Undoubtedly, $1~\%~{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}/{\mathsf{M n O}}_{\mathrm{x}}\varpi C$ composite has such a high specific surface area that it can fully touch the electrolyte, which is advantageous for improving the zinc-storage capacity. The pore diameters of the $1~\%~{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}/{\mathsf{M n O}}_{\mathrm{x}}\ @{\mathsf{C}}$ composite are basically below $30~\mathrm{{nm}}$ (Fig. S1b). The suitable pore size could expedite the zincions diffusion and enhance rate performance [48].
Scanning electron microscope (SEM) was used to examine the morphologies of as-prepared samples. The SEM images of the $1\%$ Ce-Mn-MOFs (Fig. 2a-2b) showed some fairly homogenous and non-fuzzy-three- dimensional (3D) hollow microspheres with an outer diameter of about $2\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . Furthermore, we noted that the surface of the $1\%$ Ce-Mn-MOFs was relatively rough, allowing for the observation of one-dimensional (1D) nanorods.
The impacts of the morphologies of the different initial ${\mathsf{C e/M n}}$ molar ratios of $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}/\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}@\mathsf C$ were investigated in detail. The morphologies of $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}@{\mathsf C}$ (Fig. S2) without $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ exhibited the 3D hollow microspheres with a diameter of $2.6~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ assembled from 2D nanosheets. After adding $0.5\ \%\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ , the surface of the $0.5\mathrm{~\%~}$ $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}/\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}@\mathsf C$ (Fig. S3) displayed rougher relative to the ${\mathrm{MnO}}_{\mathrm{x}}@C$ but the 3D hollow-structure microspheres with a diameter of about $1.7~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ were still assembled with 2D nanosheets.
The SEM image of $1~\%~{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}/{\mathsf{M n O}}_{\mathrm{x}}\varpi C$ (Fig. 2c) showed some 3D hollow-structure microspheres with an outer diameter of about $2\ \upmu\mathrm{m}$ and a hollow inner diameter of around $1.2~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ . The morphology and the size of the outer diameter were almost identical to the $1\%$ Ce-Mn-MOFs. This implies that the inherent structure of the sample was not disrupted by the pyrolysis technique. After carbonization treatment, it could be clearly observed that the 3D hollow-structure microspheres were visibly constructed by multiple 1D nanorods. The width and length of 1D nanorods are approximately $300\ \mathrm{nm}$ and $60\ \mathrm{nm}$ , respectively. Interestingly, all of the component nanorods’ development axes were random, resulting in a disordered surface on the 3D hollow structure microspheres. In addition, the surface of the $1~\%~{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}/{\mathsf{M n O}}_{\mathrm{x}}\varpi C$ was very coarse with abundant pores. When the amount of $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ was increased to $3\%$ it was noted that the 3D hollow microspheres $(3~\%~{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}/{\mathsf{M n O}}_{\mathrm{x}}@{\mathsf{C}})$ (Fig. S4) with an outer diameter of $2.2~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ and an inner diameter of about $1.6~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ assembled from 1D nanorods with a width of around $105~\mathrm{{nm}}$ and a length of about $315~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . When the amount of ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ was further increased to $5\%$ , the outer diameter of 3D hollow microspheres $(5\%$ $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}/\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}@\mathsf C)$ (Fig. S5) further increases to $2.8~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ , showing a hollow inner diameter of about $1.8~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ . The basis unit of 3D hollow microspheres was still 1D nanorods, with the length and width slightly changed. The width was about $108\ \mathrm{nm}$ and the length was around $327~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . It could be noted from the above results that ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ could play a significant role in the morphology and size of final materials.
The microstructure of the as-obtained $1~\%~{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}/{\mathsf{M n O}}_{\mathrm{x}}\ @{\mathsf{C}}$ was further observed by transmission electron microscope (TEM). Fig. 2d clearly displayed the hollow-structure microsphere, which was assembled from nanorods. The result was in accordance with SEM. Note that the lattice’s interplanar spacing was predicted to be 0.26, 0.31 and $0.28\ \mathrm{nm}$ by the high-resolution TEM image (Fig. 2e), which was commensurate with the pitches of the MnO (111), ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ (111) and $\mathrm{{Mn}_{3}\mathrm{{O}_{4}}}$ (20 0) planes, respectively. It should be noted that the interface between $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}$ and ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ could be clearly discerned. The results were in agreement with the XRD patterns of $1\ \%\ C e{0_{2}}/{\mathrm{MnO_{x}}\ @\mathsf{C}}.$ The matching EDX elemental mappings (Fig. 2f) for $1\ \%\ C e0_{2}/\mathrm{MnO}_{\mathrm{x}}@\mathsf C$ displayed a uniform and clear distribution of Mn, O, C and Ce elements, further demonstrating the presence of carbon and cerium elements. The EDX spectra of different molar ratios $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}/\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}@\mathsf C$ were given in Fig. S6 and Table S2 listed the concentrations of varied elements. It was obvious that the signal intensity of Ce was significantly weaker than that of Mn, which was extremely compatible with the quantity of ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ .
On the growth mechanism of hollow structure, to begin, spherical micelles of PVP were formed in a mixed solution with carbonyl groups on the exterior, which may be ascribed to the establishment of hydrogen bonds between the PVP and alcohol solution’s rich hydrophilic groups. The manganese (II) acetate and trimesic acid were then adsorbed onto the surface of spherical micelles via hydrogen bonds formed by their abundant hydroxy groups. Finally, multiple structural units progressively developed and grew firmly together, resulting in 3D hollow microspheres.[53] When the amount of $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ is increased to $1\%$ , both XRD patterns and SEM images showed that $1\ \%\ C e0_{2}/\mathrm{MnO_{x}}@C$ has obvious characteristic diffraction peaks and hollow structure assembled with 1D nanorods. However, weak diffraction peaks and interconnected nanosheets of $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}@{\mathsf C}$ and $0.5\ \%\ C e{0_{2}}/\mathrm{MnO_{x}}@C$ were revealed, suggesting the lower crystallization. The results may be caused by the introduction of a moderate mount of ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ .
The elemental compositions and chemical valence states were further characterized using $\mathsf{X}$ -ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). The C 1s peak at $284.80\mathrm{eV}$ was used to calibrate the binding energy scale from the hydrocarbon contamination. The survey spectrum (Fig. 3a) of $1\ \%\ C e0_{2}/\mathrm{MnO_{x}}@C$ further indicated the presence of Mn, O, C and Ce elements, which match the TEM-EDX elemental mappings. As shown in Fig. 3b, three main peaks of C 1s at 284.80, 285.80 and 287.37 eV, corresponding to the $\mathsf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C},\mathsf{C}{\mathrm{-}}0{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C}$ and $0-C=0$ , respectively. The detailed peaks at 641.28 and $652.83~\mathrm{eV}$ were attributable to $\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{II})2\mathrm{p}^{3/2}$ and $2{\mathsf{p}}^{1/2}$ , respectively, whilst the sub-peaks at 642.98 and 654.57 eV revealed the presence of a specific amount of $\mathsf{M n}(\mathrm{III})[57]$ (Fig. 3c). In Fig. 3d, the spin-energy separation of the Mn 3s doublet was $5.64\mathrm{eV}$ . After further calculating, the surface average oxidation state (AOS) value of Mn in $1\ \%\ C e0_{2}/\mathrm{MnO_{x}}@C$ was 2.58. The surface AOS values of Mn in $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}$ (Fig. S7a) and $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}@\mathsf C$ (Fig. S7b) were 2.28 and 2.31, respectively [58]. It agreed with XRD in demonstrating the existence of tiny $\mathrm{{Mn}}_{3}\mathrm{{O}}_{4}$ . It was noted that the high-resolution spectrum of O 1s (Fig. 3e) could be decomposed into three sub-peaks at 529.99, 531.63 and $533.39\ \mathrm{eV}$ , representing the lattice oxygen, oxygen vacancy and adsorbed oxygen, respectively [25]. Fig. 3f showed the high-resolution XPS of Ce 3d, which contained seven peaks at 882.72, 885.52, 887.89, 897.78, 900.91, 903.98 and $917.14~\mathrm{eV}$ marked as $\mathbf{v}_{1}$ , $\mathbf{v}_{2}$ , $\mathbf{v}_{3}$ , $\mathtt{v_{4}}$ , $\mathbf{u}_{1}$ , $\mathbf{u}_{2}$ and ${\bf u}_{3}$ , respectively. The five peaks $(\mathsf{v}_{1},\mathsf{v}_{3},\mathsf{v}_{4},$ $\mathbf{u}_{1}$ and $\mathbf{u}_{3}$ ) stated above were allocated to ${\mathsf{C e}}^{4+}$ , whereas the remaining two peaks ( $\mathbf{\dot{V}}_{2}$ and $\mathbf{u}_{2}$ ) were assigned to ${\mathsf{C e}}^{3+}$ . It indicated that ${\mathsf{C e}}^{4+}$ and ${\mathsf{C e}}^{3+}$ species coexist. Meanwhile, the appearance of ${\mathsf{C e}}^{3+}$ in $1\ \%\ C e0_{2}/\mathrm{MnO_{x}}@C$ showed the formation of oxygen vacancy [59].
Furthermore, the magnetic measurements were performed using an integrated physical property measurement system. (PPMS, Quantum Design-PPMS9, USA) The hysteresis loops of $1\%$ $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}/\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}\ @C$ material were recorded at different temperature (5 K, 50 K, 300 K) in magnetic fields between $^{-3}$ to $3\mathrm{T}.$ . (Fig. S8).
## 3.2. Electrochemical performance
In order to investigate the electrochemical behavior, the CV curves of $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}},$ , $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}@C$ and $1~\%~{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}/{\mathsf{M n O}}_{\mathrm{x}}\ @{\mathsf{C}}$ electrodes were performed at a scanning rate of $0.1\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ within a voltage range of $0.8\mathrm{-}1.8{\mathrm{~V}}.$ . In Fig. 4a, $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}$ electrode displayed two apparent reduction peaks located at 1.369 V and $1.182\mathrm{~V~}$ , which would be attributed to the insertion of $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}$ and $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ , respectively. While a strong oxidation peak emerged at $1.670\mathrm{V}$ , which would be attributed to the zinc-ion extraction [7,60]. Compared with $\mathrm{{MnO}_{x},}$ , the reduction peaks of $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}@\mathsf C$ shifted to high potential $(1.372\mathrm{~V~}$ and 1.186 V) and the oxidation peak shifted to low potential $(1.655\mathrm{V})$ . Compared to $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}@\mathsf C$ , the strong two reduction peaks shifted to high potential ( $\cdot1.372\mathrm{\DeltaV}$ and 1.187 V) and the oxidation peak further shifted to low potential $(1.633~\mathrm{V})$ for $1\%\ C e O_{2}/$ $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}@C$ . The voltage differential of $1~\%~{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}/{\mathsf{M n O}}_{\mathrm{x}}\varpi C$ was the lowest at $261~\mathrm{mV}$ among the three electrodes tested. It demonstrates the $1~\%~{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}/{\mathsf{M n O}}_{\mathrm{x}}\ @{\mathsf{C}}$ electrode could reveal a better rate capability of insertion and extraction of zinc ions. At the same time, the polarization could be alleviated via the ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ .
In addition, it could be further observed that the potential gap of $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}$ and $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}@\mathsf C$ was 301 and $283~\mathrm{{mV}}$ , respectively, that of $1\%$ $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}/\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}@\mathsf C$ exhibited a significant decrease to $261~\mathrm{mV}$ by GCD curves (Fig. 4b). The above results revealed that the polarization of the $1\ \%\ C e0_{2}/\mathrm{MnO_{x}}@C$ electrode material was minimum, indicating that it has superior structural stability. This would be attributed to the heterostructure could accelerate electron/ion transfer, indicating an excellent redox kinetics [61,62]. Apparently, the GCD curves exhibited one charge platform and two discharge platforms, which were compatible with the CV results. For the $1\ \%\ C e O_{2}/$ $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}@C$ electrode (Fig. 4c), with the scanning rate increasing from 0.1 to $1.0\mathrm{\mV\}s^{-1}$ , the reduction peaks gradually moved to low potential and the oxidation peak migrated to high potential, while the shape of the CV curves kept consistent. And then, the CV profiles of $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}$ , $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}@\mathsf C$ and the other different initial ${\mathsf{C e/M n}}$ molar ratios of $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}/\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}@\mathsf C$ (Fig. S9) were similar to that of $1\%C e0_{2}/\mathrm{MnO}_{\mathrm{x}}@C$ As shown in Fig. 4d, the shapes of CV curves were nearly unchanged even after 800 cycles, demonstrating an excellent structural stability during the process of zinc ions insertion and extraction rapidly. It was indicated that adding a certain amount of ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ to the electrode material could improve the cohesion of Mn-O bond, resulting in a more stable electrode material, which was consistent with the results of Raman red-shift (Fig. 1c).
The electrochemical performance of ZIBs with various electrodes was investigated to better understand the benefits of the $1~\%~{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}/{\mathsf{M n O}}_{\mathrm{x}}\varpi C$ electrode. The cycling performance of different molar ratio $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}/\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}\ @\mathsf C$ $\mathrm{(Ce/Mn~=~}0.5$ , 1, 3, 5) electrodes (Fig. S10) was compared, and the $1\ \%\ C e{0_{2}}/{\mathrm{MnO_{x}}\ @\mathsf{C}}$ electrode was chosen to explore the impacts of $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ in cells.
Fig. 5a exhibited the cycling performance of $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}$ , $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}@C$ and $1~\%~{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}/{\mathsf{M n O}}_{\mathrm{x}}\varpi C$ in a voltage range between 0.8 and $1.8{\ V}$ (vs $Z\mathrm n^{2+}/\mathrm{Zn})$ . The specific capacity of the $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathbf{x}}$ electrode was always maintained at a low level of $20\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at a current density of $500\mathrm{mAg}^{-1}$ . Compared to $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}$ , the average specific capacity of the $\mathrm{MnO}_{\mathrm{x}}@C$ electrode was obviously improved to $90\ \mathrm{mAh\{g^{-1}}}$ at the same current density. The result demonstrates that the carbon-coated could improve the electronic conductivity and ion transport, which enhanced specific capacity. In addition, it was worth noting that the specific capacity of $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}@\mathsf C$ increases slowly and progressively during the 1st-300th cycle charge process, which was attributed to the electrode’s long-term activation [39]. The slow reduction of specific capacity during the late cycle can be attributed to the slight change of electrode material structure [31]. The cycling performance curve of $1\ \%\ C e0_{2}/\mathrm{MnO_{x}}@C$ electrode exhibited a similar tendency and the capacity was higher than $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}@{\mathsf C}.$ Remarkably, the high average reversible capacity of $130\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ for the $1~\%~{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}/{\mathsf{M n O}}_{\mathrm{x}}\varpi C$ electrode was maintained during the whole 800 cycles, which exhibited superior stability. Even after 800 cycles, the capacity retention was $99.5~\%$ relative to initial charge capacity. It was attributed to the interface between ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathbf{x}}$ could accelerate electronic transfer, promoting the improvement of specific capacity. At the same time, it also showed that adding an appropriate amount of ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ into the electrode material might enhance its structural stability and be sufficient to mitigate manganese dissolution. SEM and TEM images indicate that the hollow spherical structure of $1~\%~{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}/{\mathsf{M n O}}_{\mathrm{x}}\ @{\mathsf{C}}$ electrode materials is well preserved after 400 cycles, with essentially no visible change. In addition, Mn, O, C, and Ce elements are still exist in
EDX-mappings (Fig. S11). All of these findings suggest that the $1\%$ $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}/\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}\ @\mathsf C$ electrode material has outstanding structural stability even after a lengthy cycle at $500\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ current density.
Fig. 5b exhibited the rate performance of $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}$ , $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}@C$ and $1~\%~{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}/{\mathsf{M n O}}_{\mathrm{x}}\varpi C$ electrodes. The $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}@\mathsf C$ electrode provided an average charge capacity of 117, 114, 104, 94, 85, 80, 58, 40 and $125\mathrm{\mAh\}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at 50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 1000, 2000 and $50\mathrm{mAg^{-1}}$ , respectively. By contrast, the $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}$ electrode only supplied 50 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $50\mathrm{\mA\g^{-1}}$ . When the current density was raised to $500\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , no capacity was basically achieved. It indicated that the electronic conductivity could be improved by carbon-coating, hence enhancing rate performance. Compared with the above two electrode materials, the rate properties of the $1~\%~{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}/{\mathsf{M n O}}_{\mathrm{x}}\varpi C$ electrode was obviously improved, furnishing a specific capacity of 365, 312, 255, 195, 156, 125, 92, 70 and 381 mAh $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at 50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 1000, 2000 and $50\mathrm{mAg}^{-1}$ , respectively. It demonstrates: (1) the oxygen vacancies could further enhance the electronic conductivity of the electrode material [63,64]; (2) the smaller particle size results in the shortened ion diffusion path; (3) the interface between the $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}$ facilitated the charge transfer for $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ storage. When the current density returned to $50\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , the specific capacity was slightly higher than that at the beginning. The reason was that the electrode reaction was a gradual activation process. However, the different molar amount of ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ in the electrode material have a distinct influence on the performance of the cell. For example, in the $5\ \%\ C e{0_{2}}/{\mathrm{MnO}_{\mathrm{x}}}\varpi{\cal C}$ electrode, the $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ occupied too much space volume but made almost no contribution to the storage of zinc ions, causing a reduction in specific capacity (Fig. S12).[48].
## 3.3. Electrochemical kinetics
Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was tested, which could further investigate the charge transfer rate and the rate of ion diffusion. The $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}$ $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}@C$ and $1~\%~{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}/{\mathsf{M n O}}_{\mathrm{x}}\varpi C$ electrodes were conducted by EIS within the frequency range of $10^{5}$ to $0.01~\mathrm{Hz}$ at the same potentials. To have a deeper understanding of the superior electrochemical properties of the $1\%$ $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}/\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}\ @\mathsf C$ electrode for ZIBs. Fig. 6e depicted the corresponding Nyquist plots and fitting curves. Apparently, all Nyquist plots appeared to have a semicircle in the high-medium frequency area and an angled straight line in the low-frequency region, indicating that charge transfer and ion diffusion were both controlling the electrochemical behaviour [8]. In an equivalent electrical circuit, ${\sf R}_{1}$ represented the ohmic resistance, $\mathbb{R}_{\mathrm{ct}}$ reflected the faradic charge-transfer resistance at interfaces and $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{W}}_{0}$ symbolized the Warburg impedance when $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ diffused into electrodes. It was worth noting that the better the electrode, the lower the values of the above parameters. The charge-transfer resistance (Table S3) of $1\ \%\ C e0_{2}/\mathrm{MnO}_{\mathrm{x}}@\mathsf C$ ( $\mathrm{\cdotR_{ct}}=12.47\Omega,$ was lower than that of $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}@\mathsf C$ $\mathrm{'R}_{\mathrm{ct}}=18.71\ \Omega,$ and far less than that of $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}$ $\mathrm{\cdotR_{ct}}=$ $\mathbf{68\Omega}\Omega$ ), revealing the carbon-coated in electrode materials could improve the charge transfer and electronic conductivity.
Additionally, the galvanostatic intermittent titration technique (GITT) measurements were also performed to evaluate the $\mathsf{D}_{\mathsf{Z n}}^{2+}$ of $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}$ , $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}@\mathsf C$ and $1~\%~{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}/{\mathsf{M n O}}_{\mathrm{x}}\ @{\mathsf{C}}$ . The detailed process was given in the Supporting Information. As exhibited in Fig. 6f, the $\mathsf{D}_{\mathsf{Z n}}^{2+}$ of the $1~\%~{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}/{\mathsf{M n O}}_{\mathrm{x}}\ @{\mathsf{C}}$ electrode during the charging was about $10^{-14}{-}10^{-10}\mathrm{cm}^{2}s^{-1}$ , which was higher than $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}$ (Fig. S13) and approximately $\mathrm{MnO}_{\mathrm{x}}@C$ (Fig. S14). The result indicated that oxygen vacancies could encourage electronic rearrangement and made it easier for of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions to diffuse. Apparently, the results of GITT coincided with the EIS measurements.
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, we have synthesized $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}/\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}@\mathsf C$ cathode with hollow-structure and applied it to ZIBs for the first time. Furthermore, the role of ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ was thoroughly investigated. The results demonstrated that $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ could enhance the structural stability of electrode materials while simultaneously having good electron/ ion conductivity. An excellent energy output of $365\mathrm{\mAh\}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $50\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and a long lifespan of up to 800 cycles at $500\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ could be achieved in $Z n/1\%\ C e{0_{2}}/{\mathrm{MnO_{x}}}@C$ battery. This is far superior to what was previously reported [7,66]. Therefore, the rational design of rare earth composites is a viable strategy in improving electrochemical performance. At the same time, it opens up the prospect of using rare earth composite materials in other sectors, such as electrocatalysis, supercapacitors, magnetic properties, and other system batteries.
